On Tue, 13 Oct 2026 18:33:15 -0700, Pat Maddox <pat@patmaddox.com> wrote:
> I want to adjust this a bit and do a spec-driven workflow. So the first
> goal of this thread is to produce a spec, that is implementable.

The spec is posted as "[PATCH] jails/instances/lab-mail: Initial
spec" [1]. It adds jails/instances/lab-mail/spec.org, written to stand
alone, in the same style as the lazy-jail spec. It records what you
settled here and ends with the questions I still cannot answer. Once
you have edited it, the spec-report skill can tell us whether it is
ready to implement. The reasoning you asked for is below.

>> - OpenSMTPD (mail/opensmtpd) accepts mail from the jails without
>>   auth and relays it to the provider with the stored credentials.
>
> Sounds good, but I'd like to know what alternatives there are, and make
> a deliberate choice - i.e. what's the reasoning for using opensmtpd over
> others?

The relay has to listen on SMTP, filter by envelope recipient, and
relay with auth to Fastmail. The candidates:

- OpenSMTPD. The whole job is a short smtpd.conf: one listen line, a
  table of allowed recipients, and one match rule with a relay action
  that logs in to Fastmail. Anything that matches no rule is rejected
  at RCPT TO. It is small and privilege-separated, from OpenBSD.
- Postfix. It is the most widely used and best documented, and it
  does the same job with relayhost, smtp_sasl_password_maps and
  check_recipient_access. That job is spread across main.cf and
  several lookup tables, with many more knobs than we need.
- Sendmail. It can do this, but its m4 config gives no benefit here.
- dma (base) and msmtp. Neither listens on the network; they send
  from the local machine. Each client jail would need its own copy
  with the credentials, which is what we are trying to avoid.

I chose OpenSMTPD because its config is the shortest to read and
review. Postfix would be my second choice. The one thing neither of
them does is pass Fastmail's rejection through to the client, which
you ask about below.

>> - Dovecot (mail/dovecot) with the imapc backend proxies IMAP to the
>>   provider and exposes only lists/ to the jails, read-only.
>
> ditto

For the IMAP server, Dovecot is the standard choice on FreeBSD. It
serves a maildir with a few lines of config, and it can make a
mailbox read-only. Cyrus and Courier each bring their own mail store
and admin model, which we do not need for one folder.

There is also an option with no IMAP server: nullfs-mount the local
maildir read-only into the client jails, and let notmuch read it
directly. That has fewer moving parts, but it does not give you "IMAP
available to jails", so I left it out of the spec. The spec asks how
the client jails will read the mailbox, and that answer decides it.

>> This is the part I cannot do safely with the current setup. Every
>> instance uses "ip4 = inherit", so a connection from lab-claude
>> arrives from the host's own address.
[...]
> It could listen on localhost on beastie, and thus still be accessible to
> any jails with inherit networking, right?

Right. A jail with "ip4 = inherit" uses the host's addresses,
including the real 127.0.0.1. One detail: lab-mail must also use
inherit. A jail with its own address gets its own private 127.0.0.1,
which the other jails would not reach. A dedicated address would also
work, but binding to loopback is simpler and enough.

> I don't think the client jails need to move off "inherit", do they?
[...]
> is necessary. Here's a security rule I can live with: the mail server is
> accessible via beastie, and any jails that run on it. I think we can do
> that by limiting where it binds, not necessarily having to use pf
> anywhere.

Agreed. No pf rules and no networking change; the spec states the
boundary in those words. Any local user on beastie can then send as
claude@p7x.dev and read lists/. That fits your rule, but I wanted to
spell it out.

One thing to check on the host: nothing else may already listen on
127.0.0.1:25. Sendmail did that by default before FreeBSD 14.

> p7x.dev and lists.p7x.dev is hosted on fastmail.

The spec uses imap.fastmail.com:993 and smtp.fastmail.com:465, both
implicit TLS, and an app password. Fastmail requires app passwords for
IMAP and SMTP.

> The IMAP connection should be read-only. In fact if there's a way to
> guarantee that in the mail server connection, that would be good.

As far as I know, Fastmail app passwords cannot be made read-only for
IMAP. So the guarantee has to come from lab-mail, and that decides
the next question.

> Give me an overview of the two different implementation approaches. I
> like the idea of it working even if connection to fastmail is
> down. However, the main thing I'm interested in right now is getting a
> working example going, then I can iterate on it.

imapc proxy: Dovecot forwards each client command to Fastmail as it
arrives.
- One component and no local copy. Clients see new mail at once.
- If Fastmail is down, reading stops.
- Read-only depends on Dovecot's config. Dovecot holds a read-write
  login to Fastmail, so a config mistake could let a client set flags
  or expunge on Fastmail.

mbsync copy: mbsync pulls lists/ into a local maildir on a timer, and
Dovecot serves only that copy.
- One more component, plus a cron job. New mail can take up to a
  minute to show up.
- If Fastmail is down, reading keeps working from the copy.
- Read-only holds by construction. mbsync is configured to pull only,
  and clients never talk to anything that reaches Fastmail. Whatever
  a client does, at worst it changes the local copy, and Dovecot
  makes even that read-only.

The spec uses mbsync. It is the only one of the two that gives you
the guarantee you asked for, and it also works offline. It is barely
more work for a first working example.

> The first test is pretty simple: I send an email from pat@patmaddox.com
> to lab@lists.p7x.dev. The agent reads the email and responds as
> claude@p7x.dev.

That is the spec's acceptance test, plus two negative checks: a
message to an outside address is rejected, and an expunge from a
client leaves Fastmail unchanged.

For the agent to read the mail, lab-claude needs an IMAP client that
feeds notmuch, e.g. mbsync. That means adding mail/isync to
lab-claude's packages. This is one of the spec's questions.

> - pat@patmaddox.com
> - @p7x.dev
> - @lists.p7x.dev

In the spec. Domains match exactly, so @p7x.dev does not cover
lists.p7x.dev; that is why both stay listed. Recipients are checked
one at a time. If a message goes to pat@patmaddox.com and to
someone@example.com, the second is rejected and the first still goes
out. Most MTAs behave this way. If you would rather reject the whole
message, say so and I will change the spec.

> Don't rewrite. What happens if upstream provider rejects it? Will the
> rejection be passed through to the client? That would be useful if so.

No, not with a queuing relay, and here I found a gap. OpenSMTPD (or
Postfix) puts the message in its queue and answers 250 before it
talks to Fastmail. If Fastmail then rejects the message, e.g. for a
sender it does not own, the client has already moved on. lab-mail
sends a bounce to the envelope sender, claude@p7x.dev, and that
bounce lands wherever Fastmail delivers claude@p7x.dev. If it is not
in lists/, the agent never sees it. The spec asks where that mail
lands. A Fastmail rule that files it into lists/ would close the gap.

Rejections can only reach the client directly through a proxy that
holds the client's session open while it talks to Fastmail. Dovecot's
submission service works that way. However, it needs the client to
log in, and I have not confirmed it can enforce a recipient allowlist.
Without one it would fail your outbound rule, so I did not pick it.
Before your first test, it would also help to confirm that
claude@p7x.dev is a sending identity on the Fastmail account. That
avoids the most likely rejection.

> For now it will be claude@p7x.dev since that's what we're working with.

In the spec. lab-mail does not check the sender; Fastmail decides.

